  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <para id="para-00001">In this lesson, you will compute and interpret the correlation coefficient for a
        bivariate, numerical data set.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">When you finish this lesson, you will be able to:</para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>Describe the strength of a relationship between two variables.</item>
        <item>Use technology to find the correlation coefficient and explain what the value tells
          you about a linear model in everyday language.</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00004">Here are the <emphasis>activities</emphasis> that will help you reach those goals:</para>
      <list id="list-00002">
        <item>3.5.1: Using Bivariate Data in Context</item>
        <item>3.5.2: Interpreting the Strength of Linear Relationships <list id="list-00003">
            <item>3.5.2: Self Check</item>
            <item>3.5.2: Additional Resources</item>
          </list>
        </item>
        <item>3.5.3: Describing Relationships between Two Variables <list id="list-00004">
            <item>3.5.3: Self Check</item>
            <item>3.5.3: Additional Resources</item>
          </list>
        </item>
        <item>3.5.4: Interpreting the Correlation Coefficient</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00006">After that, you’ll <emphasis>practice and review</emphasis>.</para>
      <list id="list-00005">
        <item>3.5.5: Practice</item>
        <item>3.5.6: Lesson Summary</item>
      </list>
    </section>
  </content>
